 ],
 "text": "Tonight, the moon disappears. Gone. What happens to us? I'll answer that at the very end, because first we need three smaller answers. Question one, would the oceans flood? No. The moon's pull stretches the oceans into a bulge, and that bulge is most of the tide. Without it, only the sun pulls. Tides shrink to about a third. Question two, would nights go black? Every night becomes a new moon night, and some animals tell time by moonlight. On the Great Barrier Reef, corals release their eggs together a few nights after a full moon. No moon. No calendar. Question three, would our days change? Right now, the moon is a break. Its tides drag on Earth's spin, so the day grows about two thousandths of a second longer every century. Take the moon away, and the break is gone. The day stays 24 hours. Smaller tides, darker nights, the same day. That's fine? Then why do scientists call the moon Earth's stabilizer? Because Earth spins like a top, tilted 23 and a half degrees. That tilt gives us seasons, and the moon's pull holds it steady. It barely wobbles about one degree either way. Without the moon, the tilt can drift. One famous study found it could swing from zero to 85 degrees. Newer studies say less, but still tens of degrees over millions of years. At zero, no seasons at all. At 85, one pole faces the sun for half a year. Endless day, then endless night. So what happens if the moon vanishes tonight? Tonight, almost nothing. But our far future grandchildren would live on a planet that slowly forgets its seasons. Next, what if Earth stops spinning?"
}